 "nodes": [
  {
   "id": 1,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 20,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "Visual pathway\n\nThe early visual route as a wired circuit: a retinal sheet, the optic tract, a thalamic relay and a patch of cortex. The signal reaches cortex by traveling through the anatomy, and nothing is injected into cortex directly. Every cell is one of the measured rows of Izhikevich 2007 chapter 8, so each carries a capacitance in picofarads and every current and weight in the scene is in picoamps.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": ""
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 2,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 170,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "The project nodes carry the map. A cell's position in the source population maps onto the matching position in the target, so retinotopy survives every stage. The mirror options model the inversion that a lens and the chiasm produce.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": "tracts"
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 3,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 320,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "The input node encodes contrast instead of brightness, and it splits its cells into interleaved ON and OFF mosaics the way retinal ganglion cells divide. The geometry on its mask port sets which neurons it drives.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": "stimulus"
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
